 "salt": "1w-YQums_nL9OGX-9V5gzw==",
 "text": "Poetry held a power the Celts treasured. They believed words gained force when spoken, less when written. To the Irish, poetry was a form of magic, tied to incantation and enchantment. Satire could boil a stingy king's skin or twist a thief's arm. When writing came, monks wrote down the bardic word sung aloud.",
